<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Anglo-Zulu War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1879">1879</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Zulu War · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Anglo-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulu</a> War was fought between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and the Zulu Kingdom from January to July 1879, after the high commissioner <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bartle-frere">Sir Bartle Frere</a> demanded that King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cetshwayo">Cetshwayo</a> disband his army. The Zulu destroyed a British column at Isandlwana, but were defeated at Rorke's Drift, Kambula and Gingindlovu and finally at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulundi">Ulundi</a>. Cetshwayo was captured and Zululand was divided among appointed chiefs.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/anglo-zulu-war.jpg" alt="Anglo-Zulu War" width="320" height="215" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fidodog14 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anglo-Zulu_War_collage.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulu Kingdom</a> grew from a small tribe to a large regional power under Shaka in the 1810s, and under his successors <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dingane">Dingane</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mpande">Mpande</a> it acquired two powerful new neighbours, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boers</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a>. After a civil war in the 1850s Mpande's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cetshwayo">Cetshwayo</a> became heir and de facto ruler, and he was acclaimed king in 1873 after his father died. Lord Carnarvon thought a federation in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>, as in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>, might yield a ruling white minority and a large pool of cheap labour.</p><p>In 1877 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bartle-frere">Sir Bartle Frere</a> was appointed British High Commissioner for Southern Africa to carry out such plans. A border dispute between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulu</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boers</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transvaal">Transvaal</a> was one obstacle. Sir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theophilus-shepstone">Theophilus Shepstone</a>, whom <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cetshwayo">Cetshwayo</a> regarded as a friend, first supported him, but after taking over the Transvaal as administrator he saw the matter from the Boer side and began to paint the Zulu as an aggressive threat.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Frere seized on three border incidents in 1878, including the seizure and execution of two wives of Sihayo kaXongo who had fled into Natal, and on 11 December 1878 sent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cetshwayo">Cetshwayo</a> an ultimatum. It demanded that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulu</a> army be disbanded and its military system ended, and that a British Resident be admitted. Frere delayed telling the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colonial-office">Colonial Office</a> until the full text reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> on 2 January 1879, by which time <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederic-thesiger-2nd-baron-chelmsford">Lord Chelmsford</a> had assembled more than 17,000 men on the border. The British invaded on 11 January 1879, crossing the Buffalo River at Rorke's Drift, and the war began with the Zulu victory at Isandlwana, followed by the defence of Rorke's Drift by a small garrison.</p><p>Chelmsford's second, heavily reinforced invasion advanced with extreme caution in June, building <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fortified-camp">fortified camps</a>. Victories at Kambula, Gingindlovu and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eshowe">Eshowe</a> had already restored the British position. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cetshwayo">Cetshwayo</a> tried to negotiate, but Chelmsford wished to restore his reputation before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garnet-wolseley">Sir Garnet Wolseley</a> replaced him, and on 4 July he defeated the main <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulu</a> army at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulundi">Ulundi</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulundi">Ulundi</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulu</a> army dispersed and most leading chiefs submitted. Wolseley captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cetshwayo">Cetshwayo</a> on 28 August and deposed him, discarded Frere's confederation scheme, and divided Zululand into thirteen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiefdom">chiefdoms</a> under compliant chiefs, so that the Zulu would not again unite under one king. This made internal divisions and civil wars inevitable. Chelmsford received a Knight Grand Cross of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bath">Bath</a> but was criticised by an investigation and never again served in the field, and Frere was moved to a minor post in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-town">Cape Town</a>. The British made the Zulu Kingdom a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> and annexed it in 1887.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Anglo-Zulu War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Anglo-Zulu War</b> of 1879, and especially Isandlwana and Rorke’s Drift, became famous in Britain and later in film, and it forced a reassessment of British confidence in colonial warfare.</blockquote><a href="/empire/zulu-kingdom">Zulu Kingdom · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>After the <b>Anglo-Zulu War</b> Zululand fell into civil war.</blockquote><a href="/state/nieuwe-republiek">Nieuwe Republiek</a></li><li><blockquote>The district's border with Zululand remained disputed, a grievance behind the <b>Anglo-Zulu War</b> of 1879.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-utrecht">Republic of Utrecht</a></li><li><blockquote>After the <b>Anglo-Zulu War</b> and the civil war that followed, Boers had taken the western part of Zululand as the Nieuwe Republiek.</blockquote><a href="/state/zululand-british-colony">Zululand (British colony)</a></li></ul></section>
